WEBVTT

NOTE
Landscape prototype walkthrough. These captions are also burned into the video
frame; this track exists for accessibility tooling, text extraction and reuse.

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
A first-time visitor arrives. No account, no signup wall, nothing to read first.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.500
One box. Three examples. That is the entire learning curve.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.500
They pick an example — and the analysis begins.

4
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.500
Sources appear as they are fetched. The wait shows work happening, not a spinner.

5
00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.000
The last one is greyed out — Capterra blocks automated access, so we say so rather than pretending we read it.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.500
Pricing lands first, because it is parsed from the page by code — never generated. It cannot be off by a digit.

7
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.500
A third-party price we could not confirm is shown separately — never in the table, never in the chart.

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.500
Then the written sections stream in, word by word.

9
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.500
The feature matrix has five states. A question mark means we looked and found nothing — which is a different claim from 'no'.

10
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.500
Where a competitor publishes no changelog, the report says exactly that — and lists every page it checked.

11
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.500
Every claim carries its source. One click shows the URL, the timestamp, the content hash, and the quoted line.

12
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:52.500
Now the follow-up conversation — fast, because the sources are already read.

13
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:58.500
Ask something the sources don't cover, and it says so rather than inventing an answer.

14
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.500
Anonymous users get one analysis a day — but the report itself is never degraded. Full sections, full PDF, no watermark.

15
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.500
A free account raises it to one an hour, with ten follow-ups.

16
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.500
A dollar a month gives five an hour and unlimited follow-ups. The report is identical at every tier — only frequency changes.

17
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.500
When a watched page changes, an email arrives. The subject line states the change, not the notification.

18
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.500
And the diff is the receipt behind the summary — both versions kept, both dated.

19
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:28.500
Seven community channels, all publicly readable. Sign-up and log-in issues accept posts from anyone — because someone locked out cannot log in to report it.

20
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.500
Posts stay editable after publishing, with an 'edited' marker.

21
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.500
And the operator's view: usage, capacity, quality — with every rate limit editable at runtime.

22
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.500
Change a number, and it takes effect immediately. The right values are unknowable before real traffic.

23
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:48.500
Verified sources. Honest gaps. One box to learn. That is the whole product.
